TITLE = "Correctness-Aware Self-Healing for ML Forecasting Services on Kubernetes: Detecting and Recovering from Silent Failures"

ITEMS = [
('h2', 'CHAPTER\t1\tINTRODUCTION'),
('p', "Machine-learning models are no longer research artefacts that are run once on a notebook. They are deployed as long-running network services that other programs, dashboards and people call thousands of times a day. Modern operations practice (DevOps) has produced a mature toolbox for keeping such services alive: containers package the code and its dependencies, Kubernetes schedules the containers and restarts them when they fail, and monitoring stacks such as Prometheus and Grafana make the behaviour of the running system visible. These tools were designed for ordinary web services, where a failure normally means that the service stops answering, answers with an error code, or answers too slowly."),
('p', "A machine-learning service can fail in a way that none of these tools notices. If the weights of a neural network are damaged, if an input scaler is replaced by one trained on different units, if a cache returns a stale forecast, or if a data pipeline silently changes the unit of a feature, the service continues to answer every request promptly with HTTP status 200 and a perfectly well-formed JSON document. The numbers inside the document, however, are wrong. We call this class of problem a silent failure. Because the process is running, the port is open and the response code is 200, the liveness and readiness probes that Kubernetes and Docker Compose use report the service as healthy, and the platform never intervenes. The users, or a downstream system that trusts the forecast, simply receive wrong information for as long as the fault lasts."),
('p', "This project studies silent failures in a concrete and realistic setting: the Bangalore Climate Analytics System, a Flask web application that forecasts monthly rainfall, mean temperature and humidity for the city of Bengaluru from 74 years of daily weather records (1951-2024) using two forecasting models, a seasonal ARIMA (SARIMA) model and a long short-term memory (LSTM) neural network. The original application is a single-process monolith. We re-engineer it as a containerised, Kubernetes-hosted system with a separate frontend, an inference service and a PostgreSQL database, and then use it as a test bed to answer a research question that is not answered by the standard DevOps toolbox: how can an orchestrated machine-learning service detect that its own answers are wrong, keep serving safe answers while the fault lasts, restore full quality automatically, and even predict some failures before they happen?"),
('p', "The contribution of the project is therefore twofold. First, it provides a measured characterisation of the silent-failure blind spot in default monolith, Docker Compose and Kubernetes deployments, using a purpose-built silent-fault benchmark (SilentBench) with eight injected fault types. Second, it designs, implements and evaluates a layered set of mechanisms that close the blind spot: a conformal-prediction based correctness guard, a golden-output integrity check, a cross-model canary vote, tiered graceful degradation, deadline- and load-aware degradation, a correctness service-level objective (SLO), a custom Kubernetes operator that restores full quality, a failure-precursor predictor, and a shadow-traffic gate for model delivery. Every claim made in this report is backed by an executed experiment whose raw data are stored with the project, and limitations are stated explicitly."),

('h3', '1.1  Terminology'),
('p', "The following terms are used throughout the report. They are defined once here so that the later chapters can use them without repeated explanation."),
('table', ['Term', 'Meaning in this project'], [
 ['Silent failure', 'A fault in which the service keeps answering with HTTP 200 and well-formed output, but the numerical content is wrong (for example corrupted weights, wrong scaler, stale data).'],
 ['Liveness probe', 'A Kubernetes check that decides whether a container must be restarted. By default it tests only that the process answers a trivial request.'],
 ['Readiness probe', 'A Kubernetes check that decides whether a pod receives traffic. By default it tests only that the application has started.'],
 ['Semantic health', 'Health defined by the correctness of the model output, not by process liveness. Exposed by the service on /health/semantic.'],
 ['Conformal envelope', 'A distribution-free acceptance region for forecasts, calibrated on historical windows, that bounds the false-alarm probability by the chosen level alpha.'],
 ['Window-level calibration', 'Calibrating the envelope on whole forecast windows (family-wise) instead of single values, so that the false-alarm rate refers to a complete forecast.'],
 ['Invariant', 'A property that every correct forecast must satisfy, for example that mean temperature is not constant (flat-line) across the forecast horizon.'],
 ['Cross-model canary', 'A background task that runs all models (SARIMA, LSTM, climatology) on the same horizon, aligns them by calendar month and marks a model suspect when it disagrees with the others.'],
 ['Golden output', 'The forecast produced by each model at start-up while it is known to be healthy; later outputs must reproduce it (models are deterministic).'],
 ['Tier', 'One level of the degradation chain: LSTM, SARIMA, climatology (monthly mean), cache (last valid forecast).'],
 ['Graceful degradation', 'Serving a lower-quality but still valid answer from a lower tier instead of failing or serving a wrong answer.'],
 ['Correctness SLO', 'A service-level objective stated on the fraction of served answers that are valid, estimated with a Wilson lower confidence bound and monitored with burn rates.'],
 ['MTTR / restoration time', 'Time from fault injection until full-quality answers are served again.'],
 ['Operator / CRD', 'A Kubernetes controller (built with kopf) that watches a custom resource, ForecastService, and takes recovery actions.'],
 ['Digital twin / bandit', 'A table of expected recovery cost per fault signature and action, learned from experiments, and an upper-confidence-bound bandit that chooses actions from it.'],
 ['Shadow gate', 'Evaluation of a candidate model on live traffic without serving its answers; the candidate is promoted only if its reject ratio is small.'],
 ['Deadline-aware degradation', 'A per-request time budget and per-tier concurrency caps; a tier that cannot answer in time is skipped so that a cheaper tier answers.'],
 ['SilentBench', 'The silent-fault benchmark designed in this project: eight fault types injected into four configurations, 5-10 trials per cell.'],
 ['Healthy-but-wrong', 'The state in which the platform reports the service healthy while it serves incorrect answers.'],
]),
('cap', 'Table 1.1  Terminology'),

('h3', '1.2  Scope and Relevance'),
('p', "The scope of the project covers the complete life cycle of a machine-learning inference service on a container platform: packaging (Docker), orchestration (Kubernetes on Minikube), observability (Prometheus, Grafana, OpenTelemetry with Jaeger), fault injection (custom chaos drivers, Chaos Mesh), automatic recovery (probes, a custom operator), and delivery (shadow-traffic gate, Argo Rollouts, signed images verified by an admission controller). The workload is a time-series forecasting service, but the mechanisms are described in terms of generic model outputs and can be applied to other regression-type services."),
('p', "The relevance is practical and academic. Practically, organisations increasingly run models behind Kubernetes services, and a wrong forecast that is served with a healthy status can cause real harm: a wrong rainfall forecast can mislead a flood-preparedness dashboard, and a wrong demand forecast can mislead an autoscaler that then provisions the wrong capacity. Academically, the literature on Kubernetes resilience concentrates on infrastructure faults (pod kills, node failures, latency) and on anomaly detection in infrastructure metrics, logs and traces, while the correctness of the model output is treated as outside the scope of the platform. Our review of twenty-three recent IEEE papers (Chapter 2) found that none of them evaluates correctness-aware health checking for ML outputs, which is the gap this work addresses."),
('p', "The study is deliberately bounded. It runs on a single Windows laptop with a one-node Minikube cluster, uses 5-10 trials per experimental cell, and studies one model family on one data set. It does not claim production-grade security (Appendix findings in the security review) and does not attempt multi-node scaling conclusions. These bounds are repeated in Chapter 7."),

('h3', '1.3  Motivation'),
('p', "The motivation came from a simple observation made while containerising the original application. After the monolith was split into a frontend and an inference service and deployed on Kubernetes, a fault-injection endpoint was used to corrupt the LSTM weights. The pod stayed Running, the readiness probe stayed green, Grafana showed healthy request rates and latency, and every response was HTTP 200 - yet the forecasts were nonsense. The platform that was supposed to provide self-healing had no way of knowing that anything was wrong."),
('p', "Several further observations strengthened the motivation. Reliability engineering practice defines service-level objectives on availability and latency, not on correctness, so error budgets are not consumed by wrong-but-fast answers. Existing anomaly detectors watch infrastructure signals, which are normal during a silent failure. Model-delivery practice evaluates candidate models offline, not as part of the deployment pipeline. And the few papers that combine chaos engineering with learning (for example reinforcement-learning-guided fault orchestration) optimise infrastructure recovery rather than output correctness. A systematic, measured study of silent failures in orchestrated ML services therefore seemed both necessary and feasible on modest hardware."),

('h3', '1.4  Problem Statement'),
('p', "Given a machine-learning forecasting service deployed on Kubernetes, design and evaluate a set of mechanisms that (a) detect silent failures, in which the service returns well-formed but incorrect forecasts, without relying on the model's own confidence; (b) keep the service answering with valid, clearly labelled answers while the fault persists; (c) restore full-quality answers automatically and quickly; (d) anticipate selected failures from their precursors; and (e) protect the delivery path so that a degraded model version is not promoted - while generating no false alarms on healthy operation and keeping the added latency and complexity acceptable."),

('h3', '1.5  Objectives'),
('bullets', [
 "O1. Re-engineer the monolithic Bangalore Climate Analytics application (SARIMA + real trained LSTM) into containerised services without changing the meaning or output of the original models.",
 "O2. Build a silent-fault benchmark (SilentBench) with eight injected faults and measure how monolith, Docker Compose and default Kubernetes deployments behave under it.",
 "O3. Design a correctness guard that detects silent failures with a statistically controlled false-alarm rate (conformal envelope, invariants, cross-model canary, golden-output check).",
 "O4. Implement tiered graceful degradation and a deadline- and load-aware variant so that the service never serves a wrong answer and rarely fails a request.",
 "O5. Define and monitor a correctness SLO and expose live metrics, dashboards and traces (Prometheus, Grafana, OpenTelemetry/Jaeger).",
 "O6. Implement a Kubernetes operator (CRD ForecastService) that restores full quality, and compare rule-based and learned (digital-twin + bandit) recovery policies.",
 "O7. Evaluate probe tuning (design of experiments and Bayesian optimisation), a failure-precursor predictor and a shadow-traffic delivery gate.",
 "O8. Report all results with confidence intervals and exact tests, state every limitation honestly, and package data, code and documents so that the work can be reproduced and written up as a research paper.",
]),

('h3', '1.6  Report Organisation'),
('p', "Chapter 2 surveys the literature and positions the work. Chapter 3 lists the functional, hardware and software requirements. Chapter 4 explains the underlying theory, the data set, the design methodology and the system architecture. Chapter 5 describes the implementation, key code and the testing strategy. Chapter 6 presents the results, benchmarking, screenshots and the innovative components. Chapter 7 concludes with limitations and future scope. The appendices contain a data sample and a condensed research paper in IEEE style."),

('h3', '1.7  Summary'),
('p', "This chapter introduced the problem of silent failures in orchestrated machine-learning services, defined the terminology, delimited the scope, explained the motivation and stated the problem and the objectives. The remainder of the report documents how the problem was characterised, how it was solved with a layered set of mechanisms, and how well the solution works."),
]
